---
import '@fontsource-variable/roboto-serif';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ContactForm from '../components/ContactForm.astro';
import { rates, gbp } from '../data/rates';
import { forwardDeployedFaqs } from '../data/faqs';
import FaqList from '../components/FaqList.astro';


const serviceSchema = {
  '@type': 'Service',
  '@id': 'https://intentionaut.com/forward-deployed/#service',
  name: 'Product building',
  serviceType: 'Embedded product prototyping',
  description:
    'Embedded product building: three days a week inside your team, building in your environment on your problems, ending in working software your team can take over or a prototype that tells them whether to build it.',
  provider: { '@id': 'https://intentionaut.com/#person' },
  areaServed: { '@type': 'Country', name: 'United Kingdom' },
  offers: {
    '@type': 'Offer',
    priceCurrency: 'GBP',
    price: rates.forwardDeployed,
    // unitText is not an Offer property; the per-day unit lives on the price spec.
    priceSpecification: {
      '@type': 'UnitPriceSpecification',
      price: rates.forwardDeployed,
      priceCurrency: 'GBP',
      unitText: 'day',
    },
    availability: 'https://schema.org/LimitedAvailability',
  },
};

const faqSchema = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: forwardDeployedFaqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.answer.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim() },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Product building | Saielle DaSilva"
  description="Embedded product building for teams that cannot get a working version of the idea in front of anyone: three days a week in your environment, building on your problems, ending in working software your team can take over."
  jsonLd={[serviceSchema, faqSchema]}
  ogImage="/og/forward-deployed.png"
>
  <section class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Build &amp; delivery</p>
      <h1>The prototype has been two sprints away for four months.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        You want to make bigger bets but don't have capacity to build.
      </p>
      <ul class="problem-lines">
        <li>Your AI proof of concept runs on one laptop, nowhere near your real data.</li>
        <li>Three people describe the same feature three different ways, and all three are right.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="how-line">
        I run the build, in your repo and on your data. Along the way I connect
        the people, the technology and the language around it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="prose" aria-labelledby="example-h">
      <h2 id="example-h">A real example</h2>
      <ul class="stat-strip example-stats" aria-label="One engagement in numbers">
        <li>
          <span class="stat-num">3.7 days</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Per release, down from 7.7 days</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="stat-num">80%+</span>
          <span class="stat-label">New user adoption</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="stat-num">Weekly</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Customer contact, up from quarterly</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="stat-num">54%</span>
          <span class="stat-label">Decrease in agentic coding errors, from 68% to 31%</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        One client validated a platform bet for their data stack in 48 hours,
        then worked with me for four weeks. We built a prototype experience in
        two days and showed the concept could work, and the longer build took a
        few weeks. The team has built confidence and does more user research.
        I taught them how to build with AI, getting them to work through issues
        daily and improving their AI native ways of working as a team.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        You can describe the problem; you can nearly describe the answer. What
        you cannot do is get a working version of it in front of anyone,
        because everyone who could build it is already committed to this
        quarter.
      </p>
      <p>
        So it stays a document, discussed in one meeting, then a
        different meeting. Six weeks later the argument is still about what it
        might do, because nobody has watched it do anything. That is not a
        prioritisation failure. It is what happens when the only way to test an
        idea is to spend a quarter building it properly.
      </p>

      <h2>What I do</h2>
      <p>
        I embed with your team for a fixed block, three days a week, and build
        in your environment: your repo, your stack, your data, your
        constraints. Part of the work is getting the team to say out loud what
        they intend, because that is usually the thing nobody has written
        down. Then I build against it.
      </p>
      <p>
        You will get a summary deck to help your stakeholders understand what's
        at stake and why this work matters, but you also get a working prototype
        a team can take on, and working code in your environment under your
        rules.
      </p>
      <p>
        And if the answer is "you shouldn't build that," I'll tell you. It's
        cheaper to hear that in week two than month six. Close the gap on your
        opportunity costs and get the clarity you need to make informed
        decisions.
      </p>

      <h2>What you get</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Working software in your environment, not a demo in mine</li>
        <li>A codebase your team can take over, extend or bin without asking me</li>
        <li>The failure modes you would otherwise have found in month six</li>
        <li>A real estimate of what finishing it costs, in time and people</li>
        <li>A straight recommendation: keep going, change the shape, or stop</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        It starts with a two-day pilot. I come into your environment, work
        with your team on a sample of your real data, and by the end of day two
        we compare the new results to the prior ones. {gbp(rates.fdePilot)}
        fixed price. If the idea can't be honestly attempted in two days, I'll
        tell you before we start.
      </p>
      <p>
        If it is a fit, you get a written proposal: what is worth prototyping,
        what it would take, and what it costs. You can stop there. Some people
        should.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then a block of four to twelve weeks, three days a week, at
        {' '}{gbp(rates.forwardDeployed)} a day. Four days a week is possible
        at the same daily rate, though three is the default. The day away is
        where the thinking happens.
      </p>
      <p>
        It ends on a date agreed at the start. What exists afterwards is
        something that runs, which your team can take over, extend or bin on
        evidence, and enough written reasoning that the next person to touch it
        knows why it is shaped the way it is. I do not sell a support tail.
      </p>

      <h2>What the two days test</h2>
      <p>
        Things regress and quality slips, and teams do not know until they hear
        from their users. I have been building a framework for applying
        deterministic rules to probabilistic outputs, and pipelines for
        decision provenance and observability, and using them for myself and my
        clients for months. The pilot puts them on a sample of your real data.
      </p>

      <h2>What this is not</h2>
      <p>
        How far it goes depends on the problem. Some blocks end with a
        prototype that answers the question and should then be rebuilt
        properly. Some end with software your team can take over and run. What
        I will not do is hand you the first and call it the second. You will
        know which one you are getting within two to four weeks, not at the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        It will not replace a product leader either. If the real problem
        is that nobody senior owns product direction, prototyping around that
        gets you a good demo and the same argument. That is
        {' '}<a href="/fractional/">fractional and interim leadership</a>, and
        it is a different engagement.
      </p>

      <h2>What I have built</h2>
      <p>
        In companies: I led the product and design organisation at StepStone,
        where the satellite portfolio returned 3.2x. At Sainsbury's I ran
        product for AI, data platforms and machine learning, and took
        production AI from a handful of experiments to a platform serving over
        100 million customers. I was on the founding team at Cazoo, through to
        a $7B IPO. As interim CPO at a direct-to-consumer grocery scaleup,
        monthly recurring revenue grew 450 per cent in 90 days. Founding hire
        at Pivotal; Amazon and AT&amp;T before that.
      </p>
      <p>
        On my own: Friday, a coaching product for early-career film and
        television crew. Familiar, a set of writing tools. Kernic, a command
        line tool that turns a few answers into a design system. Each was
        built and released in days rather than quarters, and each is on the
        {' '}<a href="/projects/">projects page</a> with what it bet on and how
        it was made.
      </p>
      <p>
        The mix is the point. You are getting someone who has run the function
        that would own this afterwards, and who still writes the code.
      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="mailto:hello@intentionaut.com?subject=FDE%20pilot">Start the pilot</a>
    </div>

    <section class="contact-form-section" aria-label="Get in touch">
      <div class="contact-card">
        <h2>Let's talk.</h2>
        <p class="form-lede">
          If there is something you need to see working before you can decide
          about it, tell me what it is.
        </p>
        <ContactForm source="forward-deployed" selected="forward-deployed" booking />
      </div>
    </section>

    <FaqList
      items={forwardDeployedFaqs}
      sub="Straight answers about cost, attention, what happens if it is not working, and what this does not do."
    />
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .example-stats {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }
  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .example-stats {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
